the worst moment in russia's naval  history since world war ii  its long-serving flagship sunk with it  russian pride and its perception of  naval invincibility in the black sea  it's a boost for those defending  odessa's shores  i believe that our army can do  everything  and  as you saw russian  great army is not great army  and we have great people and great  soldiers and we're ready to defend  ourselves  the moskva was built 70 kilometers up  the coast from here in the ukrainian  town of mikhail and it was authorities  here in odessa who were first to say  that it had been struck by two ukrainian  neptune missiles ultimately sending it  to the bottom of the sea  moscow claims it sank after a fire it's  not clear how many of the 500 crew were  killed but ukraine claimed that the  captain was among the dead odessa is  just glad it's gone everybody's talking  about this and  we are really happy everybody  celebrating i want to believe that our  at least this location odessa and the  odessa region will be a little bit safer  because that's one of the  worship that was  threatening us  commissioned during the cold war the  moskva was fitted with anti-ship and  anti-aircraft missiles  there's speculation that nuclear  warheads might have gone down with it  that would mean a big recovery operation  for russia  but the flagship's loss is also a  strategic headache  it also warns them that they've got to  stand off the ukrainian coast a bit  further because they didn't take  ukrainian missile capabilities seriously  because they hadn't seen them before and  now they will be worried about the  neptune missiles they don't know and we  don't know how many of these the  ukrainians have got  the last satellite images show the  moskva dwarfing two other ships in  crimea's port of sevastopol on the 7th  of april then at sea on the 10th but the  moment it entered the theater of war in  late february it became a symbol of  ukrainian mockery  at snake island the warship tannoyed  